<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coenwulf of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/796">796</a>–821</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coenwulf was king of Mercia from 796 to 821, a distant kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>. He crushed a revolt in Kent in 798, captured its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-praen">Eadberht Præn</a> and later made his own brother king there, keeping Mercia the strongest of the English kingdoms. His long quarrel with Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wulfred">Wulfred of Canterbury</a> over church lands troubled his last years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coenwulf-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Coenwulf of Mercia" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scan: British Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coenwulf,_King_of_Mercia,_gold_mancus;_struck_796%E2%80%93821_AD_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Coenwulf was a descendant of King Pybba, who ruled Mercia in the early 7th century. His family was powerful but not of recent Mercian royal lineage, and he is recorded witnessing charters under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>. Offa died in July 796 and was succeeded by his son Ecgfrith, who reigned for less than five months before Coenwulf took the throne. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a> regarded him as a tyrant. His early reign saw a breakdown of Mercian control in the south: in East Anglia a king named Eadwald issued his own coins, and in Kent an uprising was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-praen">Eadberht Præn</a>, who had been an exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s court and probably had Carolingian support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Coenwulf would not act in Kent without word from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> that Eadberht was a pretender, because Eadberht had reportedly been a priest. The pope's authorisation came in 798. The Mercians captured Eadberht, put out his eyes and cut off his hands. By 801 Coenwulf had placed his brother Cuthred on the throne of Kent, and when Cuthred died in 807 he took the kingdom himself, styling himself in 809 King of the Mercians and the Province of Kent. His own coinage in East Anglia began by about 805, so he probably restored Mercian control there by force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> stayed independent under Egbert, so he never held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>'s overlordship over the southern English.</p><p>He fought the Welsh, killing Caradog ap Meirion of Gwynedd by 798, and later ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> and devastated Dyfed. He quarrelled with Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wulfred">Wulfred of Canterbury</a> over lay control of religious houses, and by 821 he was threatening to exile him unless he surrendered an estate of 300 hides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Coenwulf died in 821 at Basingwerk, probably while preparing a campaign against the Welsh, and was buried at Winchcombe. His brother Ceolwulf succeeded him but was deposed within two years, and the kingship passed permanently out of his family. A gold mancus bearing his name, found at Biggleswade in 2001, was bought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 2006. He was the last Mercian king to exercise substantial dominance over other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms, and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> under Egbert began within a decade of his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coenwulf of Mercia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Coenwulf</b> (796–821) held much of it together and crushed a revolt in Kent in 798, but disputed successions followed, and in 825 Ecgberht of Wessex defeated King Beornwulf at the Battle of Ellendun.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Basingwerk Abbey, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.29,-3.21&amp;year=808" aria-label="Basingwerk Abbey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4479 330 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Coenwulf of Mercia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4929 600h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4929 600h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.29,-3.21&amp;year=808">What was here in 808 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
